WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:01.005
(metal music)

00:00:01.005 --> 00:00:04.900
- The year is 1988; big
hair and denim are in.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.578
("Seventeen" by Winger)

00:00:07.578 --> 00:00:10.256
The ultimate lead singer
in hair metal, Kip Winger,

00:00:10.256 --> 00:00:13.139
and his band are at the top of the charts.

00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:15.731
But the party that seemed
like it would never end

00:00:15.731 --> 00:00:18.087
suddenly came crashing down.

00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:20.596
(birds singing)

00:00:20.596 --> 00:00:23.346
No air play, no singles, nothing.

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:26.831
But it turns out this
was just the beginning

00:00:26.831 --> 00:00:28.348
for Kip Winger.

00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:31.094
- This is, uh, you know, at
home with Kip Winger.

00:00:31.094 --> 00:00:32.409
(laughs)

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:34.492
This is so exciting.

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:36.830
(banjo music)

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.462
- I'm Chris Funk.

00:00:38.462 --> 00:00:41.103
I'm a musician in a band
called The Decemberists,

00:00:41.103 --> 00:00:43.481
and I'm on a journey looking
for the most surprising

00:00:43.481 --> 00:00:45.786
and extraordinary people in music.

00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:49.762
My next stop takes me
to Nashville, Tennessee.

00:00:49.762 --> 00:00:52.542
There I will meet a rock
legend with one of the most

00:00:52.542 --> 00:00:55.542
unexpected transformations in music.

00:00:57.509 --> 00:00:58.708
- Hi guys, we're The Decemberists.

00:00:58.708 --> 00:01:00.716
We're from Portland, Oregon,
United States of America.

00:01:00.716 --> 00:01:03.084
(audience cheers)

00:01:03.084 --> 00:01:04.835
A Decemberists tour
brought me to Nashville

00:01:04.835 --> 00:01:08.418
where we played the
historic Ryman Theater.

00:01:09.793 --> 00:01:12.471
A day between shows gave me
a chance to explore the city

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:15.888
known for its honky tonks and live shows.

00:01:17.185 --> 00:01:19.422
But I was here for something
else; to track down

00:01:19.422 --> 00:01:20.422
an ‘80s icon.

00:01:22.587 --> 00:01:25.040
- What's up?
- Hello.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.417
- I'm Kip Winger, and I'm
in the band, Winger, and I'm

00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:30.584
also a classical composer.

00:01:33.342 --> 00:01:36.244
At the height of my career,
we were playing for thousands

00:01:36.244 --> 00:01:40.411
of people every night and
gold and platinum albums.

00:01:41.981 --> 00:01:44.947
Beavis and Butthead was the
biggest show in MTVs history

00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:46.527
at the time, and it ushered in a whole

00:01:46.527 --> 00:01:48.777
new genre of music, grunge.

00:01:50.751 --> 00:01:54.888
And it completely ripped
apart the ‘80s hair bands,

00:01:54.888 --> 00:01:56.779
and I was at the scene of the crime.

00:01:56.779 --> 00:01:59.914
My name was on the little
geeky guy's T-shirt

00:01:59.914 --> 00:02:03.259
that represented everything that sucked.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:05.726
Lost the record deal, lost the publishing.

00:02:05.726 --> 00:02:07.692
I was at my very lowest point.

00:02:07.692 --> 00:02:10.120
I did say, well there's
nothing else to do now

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:10.953
except that thing that
I've always wanted to do,

00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:12.606
(subtle bagpipe music)

00:02:12.606 --> 00:02:16.773
which is really learn how to
wield an orchestra organically.

00:02:22.173 --> 00:02:24.981
- To go from a rock bass
player to composer is learning

00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:26.958
music all over again.

00:02:26.958 --> 00:02:31.125
To do so later in life, as
Kip did, is almost unheard of.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.033
(classical music)

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.206
- I could never even
read an orchestral score

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:46.456
until I was 35.

00:02:48.068 --> 00:02:51.296
10, 15 years into it to
finally start to hear

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:54.425
orchestras play my music,
and realize, oh my God,

00:02:54.425 --> 00:02:57.842
this is really what I'm here to be doing.

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:00.694
- Dude, that's awesome.

00:03:00.694 --> 00:03:01.753
Amazing.

00:03:01.753 --> 00:03:04.538
- In this case I took a,
I'm calling it a riff,

00:03:04.538 --> 00:03:08.766
'cause that's my terminology
from being a rock guy.

00:03:08.766 --> 00:03:11.183
(piano riff)

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.128
(musical sounds) turns into...

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:19.045
(orchestral music)

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:24.403
It was a scary and lonely path.

00:03:24.403 --> 00:03:26.257
I've got a record company
that's going, why are

00:03:26.257 --> 00:03:27.331
you doing that?

00:03:27.331 --> 00:03:28.410
You're a rocker.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.664
You need to rock.

00:03:29.664 --> 00:03:31.681
And I'm like, no, no, no, I
don't need to rock; I need

00:03:31.681 --> 00:03:33.880
to be doing this.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.195
This just kind of starts
all on a rock riff, really.

00:03:37.195 --> 00:03:39.113
You know, you kinda go
against all odds when you

00:03:39.113 --> 00:03:41.280
just believe in something.

00:03:43.965 --> 00:03:46.398
- Despite the challenges
with learning how to compose,

00:03:46.398 --> 00:03:49.548
Kip's persistence ultimately
led to a Grammy nomination

00:03:49.548 --> 00:03:52.014
in Contemporary Classical Composition.

00:03:52.014 --> 00:03:54.764
(pleasant music)

00:03:55.809 --> 00:03:59.796
- It's very cathartic,
healing experience to overcome

00:03:59.796 --> 00:04:03.720
my illiteracy in music
at a late age, with not

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.791
so much self-confidence.

00:04:05.791 --> 00:04:07.642
It was like, okay, you know what?

00:04:07.642 --> 00:04:08.725
I don't suck.

00:04:09.576 --> 00:04:13.042
Like, it wasn't true, what they all said.

00:04:13.042 --> 00:04:15.584
To be acknowledged with
a composer nomination

00:04:15.584 --> 00:04:18.948
from the Grammys was the greatest feeling

00:04:18.948 --> 00:04:21.678
of accomplishment of my whole life.

00:04:21.678 --> 00:04:24.428
(pleasant music)

